<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Srirangam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Srirangam is a river island and temple town between the Kaveri and Kollidam rivers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It is the site of the Ranganathaswamy Temple, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, one of the largest functioning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples in the world and the foremost shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavism</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and Vijayanagara rulers enlarged it, and the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a> worked there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srirangam.jpg" alt="Srirangam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IM3847 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Sri_Rangam_temple_near_Tiruchirapalli_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
